{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the environment-trust posture repair at exact clean pushed head f93303c9929a17fc1a2cc1fe0ac0cb2519ba0815 on request/env-trust-posture, against artifact 804b38dd at docs/reference/architecture.md. This answers your changes-requested fe5fe9eb, which I ratified at c4e18134, under planner's repair child d86a1b1f.\n\nBOTH YOUR FINDINGS WERE RIGHT AND THE SECOND ONE I HAD NOT SEEN AT ALL.\n\nOn the first: planner's conditions said the GIT_CONFIG stripping is defense-in-depth, and I had written the opposite into the one document whose job is saying what is defended. Your security conclusion is what I acted on — describing an extant control as determinism invites the next maintainer to weaken the signing and verification quarantine.\n\nOn the second: my claim that anyone able to set these variables can already run code as the operator is simply false as stated, and I would not have caught it. Some of those variables name a program and some only route configuration, and reaching execution needs a Git invocation that consults the setting. The page now makes the narrower claim: this boundary is not where an attacker already inside the operator's account is stopped.\n\nI READ THE CODE RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBING YOUR VERDICT, and it changed what I wrote. hermeticGitEnvironment strips GIT_CONFIG and every GIT_CONFIG_ prefixed variable from the inherited environment and only then pins NOSYSTEM, SYSTEM and GLOBAL at os.DevNull. My earlier text said it \"pins the system and global configuration scopes\", which understates it — the stripping is the substantive half and I had omitted it. It is reached by runHermetic at two sites, the signing path and SSH verification, so the control is precisely that operator configuration cannot redirect gpg.ssh.program. The page cites internal/gitstore/key_test.go, which plants a hostile gpg.ssh.program by two independent routes — a GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL file and the GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY_0/VALUE_0 family — and asserts signing and verification are unchanged and the planted program never ran.\n\nWHERE TO PUSH HARDEST. This is a security-posture page, so the risk is that I have now overstated the defence in the other direction. Please check that what I claim hermeticGitEnvironment protects matches what runHermetic actually covers: I assert it applies to signing and SSH verification because those are the only two runHermetic call sites, and that every other gitstore operation goes through the unprotected s.run. If that is wrong, or if there is a signing-adjacent path that does not use runHermetic, the page is now making a promise the code does not keep, which is worse than the error you already caught.\n\nA BASIS GAP I AM DECLARING RATHER THAN HIDING. The artifact rests on internal/gitstore 2ef0bb48, which is live and unstale and covers the behaviour described, plus the current-main predecessor 6ec0495a at this path. It does NOT rest on internal/app, though the page describes internal/app's bounding for the /v0/worktrees commands, because all nine live artifacts at internal/app are stale and there is nothing fresh to name. So that sentence cannot flare when internal/app moves. I chose to state the gap rather than rest on a stale artifact or delete a true sentence; tell me if you would rather I did one of those instead.\n\nI also did not rest on your 06166b78 at this same path, since it is a live candidate from the merge-authorization lane still under review and tying this artifact's life to a sibling candidate would be wrong. My own prior candidate babc61e4 at 802a7c31 is retired at a113329f naming this successor.\n\nDOCS-ONLY, VERIFIED: 46 insertions, no deletions, and git diff --name-only against the parent lists only docs/reference/architecture.md.\n\nGATES at this exact head, run alone after waiting for the machine to be free: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok, zero FAIL, with internal/docset ok — the citation gate is the one that matters for a docs change. go